The seam: what happens at a boundary that is both axes at once

You noted the fluid boundary and the genetic boundary are the same boundary — and called that coincidence the tell that the axes are unified. It mostly is. But "same boundary, two jobs" hides a sequencing question that will bite in implementation, and it's better to expose it now than to let a sub-agent paper over it.

When two cells touch, two processes want to run across that interface: rheological pressure-balance (they share space, menisci form, the diffusion ∂φ/∂t = D∇²φ blends their material fields) and genetic exchange (traits flow, symbiosis). The seam is: these have different natural timescales and can disagree. Pressure-balance is fast — a frame or two. Gene exchange should probably be slow, deliberate, maybe even gated by consent (you don't want two cells fusing genomes just because they brushed past each other during a reflow).

If we let one boundary run both at one rate, we get a bug that looks like a feature until it ruins a layout: cells that graze each other during settling start swapping data. The fix is clean and worth stating as spec now — the boundary carries two independent coupling constants, not one. Call them D (the material diffusion / assimilation dial you already defined) and G (gene-exchange permeability). They live on the same interface but are separate scalars:

* D governs do these two regions blend their physical material (always at least slightly on — that's what makes it one medium).
* G governs do these two regions exchange genome (default 0 — cells touch without merging identity; symbiosis is opt-in by raising G).

This preserves your "same boundary" elegance — it's still one interface — while preventing physical proximity from forcing genetic fusion. It also gives symbiotic binding a precise definition: binding is raising G on a shared interface, nothing more. And it protects mitosis: division is the reverse — a boundary forms inside one cell with D pinching it apart while the genome is copied, not exchanged. Two operations, one boundary primitive, distinguished by which coupling constant is engaged. That's the axes staying unified without collapsing into an ambiguity. Adding G to the material state.

The full per-point/per-interface state is now: point state {S, n, λ, skin}, interface state {D, G}. Still tiny. Still the token-economy argument intact.
