What the debugging actually taught us (this is the real value)
The paper was coherent, but building it surfaced four things the math alone didn't:

Non-penetration must be stiff regardless of material. My first instinct — scale contact compliance by softness — was wrong and it jammed: soft regions interpenetrated and never separated (724 persistent overlaps). Softness belongs in internal deformation (cohesion/turgor), not in whether two regions can occupy the same space. This is a genuine correction to the model's engineering.
"Overdamped" means little carried momentum, not heavy damping. I had it backwards (retaining 90% velocity → sloshing). Overdamped gradient flow wants momentum to barely persist.
The thixotropic ξ mechanism is load-bearing, not decorative. Frictionless frustrated packings creep forever — every production physics engine solves this with a sleep system. The paper already has the physically-correct one: structure rebuilds at rest, raising yield until sub-yield creep is arrested. Wiring ξ into actual motion-arrest is what made it settle cleanly. The metaphor turned out to be the mechanism.
Initial conditions matter enormously. A jammed clump is pathological; a jittered grid makes settling a well-posed relaxation. The settled layout is clean — min inter-region centroid distance ~4.9 for ~4-unit blobs, i.e. properly tiled, not collapsed.

